<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="farnesene biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5725" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The C15 isoprenoid farnesenes are a group of closely related sesquiterpenoids that occur as &amp;alpha;- and &amp;beta;- isomers. The &amp;alpha;-farnesene can exist in four isomeric forms, |FRAME: CPD-8764|, (E,Z), (Z,Z), and |FRAME: CPD-9338|, of these, the most abundant one is (E,E)-&amp;alpha;-farnesene. The (E)-&amp;beta;-farnesene is the only naturally occurring one. |FRAME: CPD-8764| is produced by a number of plant tissues in response to herbivory, wounding, pathogen and general defense |CITS: [15896363]|. However, |FRAME: CPD-8764| is also emitted as an attractant in codling moth and is a sex pheromone in mice |CITS: [1798765]|. |FRAME: CPD-8239| on the other hand is regarded as an alarm pheromone of aphids and wild potato (S. barthaultii) releases this compound from its trichomes as a defense against aphids |CITS: [GIBSON83]|. Biosynthesis of |FRAME: CPD-8764| has been extensively studied in apples as they cause a serious physiological condition called scald, affecting the commercial aspects of fruit storage and marketability |CITS: [14740213]|. Accumulation of |FRAME: CPD-8764| occurs in the the epidermal and hypodermal cell layers of fruits, and high levels are reported in the epicuticular coating of scald-susceptible apples during the first weeks of storage |CITS: [14740213]|. Farnesenes are synthesized via the |FRAME: PWY-922| through |FRAME: PWY-5123| and the final, rate limiting enzyme (farnesene synthase - this pathway) converts |FRAME: FARNESYL-PP| into farnesene |CITS: [Rupasinghe01]|. Farnesene is also naturally produced by |FRAME: TAX-4932| via the mevalonate pathway. It can be chemically hydrogenated to produce farnesane which is being evaluated as a potential biofuel |CITS: [22895337]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="O-27" />
    <Author name="pujar" />
  </Authors>
